From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yevhen Salitrynskyi <ysalitrynskyi@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 19:51:24 -0500
Subject: feat: pins and essentials in every window (ADR 0035)

A pinned tab or an essential was a tab of the window it was pinned in. A
second window of the profile shared the Spaces but not their pins, so a
pin made there was missing here, and the same pin in two windows was two
pins to sync. Arc's pins are the profile's: every window shows them.

Pins and essentials are now the profile's (windows G2, G13-G22). A
profile service, PinStore, keeps one record per Space pin and per
essential in the preference stedding.pins.store: its id (the tab token
sync already uses), its tier, its Space, its home, its own name, its
separator, and for a pin in a pinned folder the folder's path as the
session writes it. Each window that follows the registry has a PinMirror
(a window feature). It publishes what changed in its own pins a turn
after the change, and brings its tabs in line with the store: a tab of
each record, asleep until chosen there, in the record's Space, folders
and order. Each window's tab is a page of its own (G14).

A record removed in one window closes the asleep copies elsewhere
without a trace and leaves an open one as a loose tab (G15). A new
window has every pin a turn after it opens; a pin moved to another
window takes its page there; closing a window removes nothing (G16).
"Switch to this tab" for a pin's page goes to this window's own tab of
it, and the command bar lists no other window's tab of a pin (G17). A
restart matches restored pins to records by id (G18). The first launch
with the store fills it from every restored window, after a backup of
each sidebar, merging the same pin found in two windows (G19). Sync sees
one item per pin whatever the number of windows, an import reaches every
window (G20), and a deleted Space's pins move once (G21).

A window with many records to make (more than 40) makes them a Space at
a time, a task each, the essentials and the Space on screen first. Each
Space pin comes in already filed, as a restored tab does, at its place
in the run, and the sidebar lays out and redraws its rows once per
Space rather than once per tab (SpaceModel::ScopedBulkFiling,
TabCollectionAnimatingLayoutManager::ScopedDeferTargets). An asleep tab
shows the icon history keeps for its page, or its site's (S-496). With
443 pins a new window took about 10 s with the browser standing still;
its own Space now comes in under a second and no task takes more than
about 0.7 s (S-546).

Why: GitHub issue 111; the owner chose option B of ADR 0035
(2026-11-15) for 0.6.0 (PLAN decision 23). Spec: windows G2, G13-G22.
Removable when: never; Stedding's own model of pins.
